<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Géza II of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1130">1130</a>–<a href="/years/1162">1162</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Géza II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1141 to 1162. He defended the kingdom against German and Byzantine pressure, intervened repeatedly in the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, and invited German settlers, later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a>, to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. Flemish, Walloon and other western 'guests' also settled in Hungary during his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geza-ii-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Géza II of Hungary" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Márk Kálti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%A9pes_kr%C3%B3nika_-_117.oldal_-_II._G%C3%A9za_uralkod%C3%B3i_d%C3%ADszben.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Géza was born in 1130, the eldest son of Béla the Blind and Helena of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. His father had been blinded in the 1110s, together with his own father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almos-duke-of-croatia">Álmos</a>, on the orders of King Coloman. Béla became king in 1131, and Queen Helena took Géza and his younger brother Ladislaus to an assembly at Arad, where she ordered the killing of sixty-eight noblemen who had counselled the blinding, according to the Illuminated Chronicle. Béla died on 13 February 1141, and Géza, aged eleven, was crowned on 16 February. His mother and her brother Beloš ruled as regents in the first years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In early 1146 the pretender Boris Kalamanos, with German mercenaries recruited with the consent of Conrad III, took the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>. The royal forces blockaded it and the mercenaries surrendered. Géza, sixteen and girded with a sword as a sign of coming of age, led an army across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leitha">Leitha</a> and routed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-duke-of-austria">Henry Jasomirgott</a>, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, in the Battle of the Fischa on 11 September. He married Euphrosyne, sister of Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iziaslav-ii-of-kiev">Iziaslav II of Kiev</a>, in the second half of that year. In 1147 he paid money to the Germans and the German crusaders crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> without major incident. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> refused to hand Boris over but took him out of Hungary.</p><p>Between 1148 and 1155 Géza intervened at least six times in the struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> on behalf of Iziaslav II, sending reinforcements or leading troops himself. He sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> against Conrad III and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, who restored their suzerainty over Serbia. Peace treaties with Manuel were signed in about 1150 and 1151, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Sardica</a> in 1153 and again in 1155. He invited Western European knights, primarily German, to settle, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diploma-andreanum">Diploma Andreanum</a> of 1224 says the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a> were invited by him. He also employed Muslim soldiers recruited from the peoples of the steppes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Géza's brothers Stephen and Ladislaus fell out with him and fled to the court of Manuel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. From 1158 to 1160 Géza sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> to help Frederick I against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>. He first recognised Victor IV as pope in 1160, then changed sides to Alexander III after most European monarchs had done so, and in 1161 signed a concordat with Alexander. Shortly before his death he granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and other lands to his younger son Béla as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> duchy. He died on 31 May 1162 and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a>. His eldest son Stephen succeeded him, and Béla later inherited the kingdom after Stephen's death in 1172.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Géza II of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…as frontier warriors, Muslim and Jewish merchants handled trade and sometimes royal finances, and from the twelfth century Germans, Walloons and Italians were invited as ‘guests’ (hospites) with their own liberties; <b>Géza II</b> settled the Saxons of Transylvania, whose rights Andrew II confirmed in the Andreanum of 1224.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tolna, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.43,18.78&amp;year=1150" aria-label="Tolna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Géza II of Hungary"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4343 6094h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4343 6094h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.43,18.78&amp;year=1150">What was here in 1150? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
